---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Reference Overview', href: '/docs/reference' },
  { title: 'Glossary', href: '/docs/reference/glossary', active: true },
  { title: 'Known Issues', href: '/docs/reference/known-issues' },
  { title: 'Roadmap', href: '/docs/reference/roadmap' },
  { title: 'Changelog', href: '/docs/reference/changelog' },
  { title: 'Literature', href: '/docs/reference/literature' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'core', text: 'Core Concepts', level: 2 },
  { id: 'detection', text: 'Detection Terms', level: 2 },
  { id: 'architecture', text: 'Architecture Terms', level: 2 },
  { id: 'scoring', text: 'Scoring Terms', level: 2 },
  { id: 'operations', text: 'Operations Terms', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Glossary" description="Definitions of key terms and concepts" section="Reference" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Core concepts" href="#core" icon="book" tag="Start here" description="Honeypot, IDS, SIEM, CVE and WSTG — the vocabulary the rest of the documentation assumes." />
    <DocCard title="Detection terms" href="#detection" icon="radar" tag="Signals" description="SID, risk score, threshold, decay, permaflag, offense, honeytoken and GDPR signature." />
    <DocCard title="Architecture terms" href="#architecture" icon="layers" tag="Modules" description="Pure module, adapter module, pool, escalation and sticky routing." />
    <DocCard title="Scoring terms" href="#scoring" icon="gauge" tag="Decisions" description="Stage 6, shared address, IP reputation cap and decision trace." />
    <DocCard title="Operations terms" href="#operations" icon="wrench" tag="Running it" description="Hardening, runbook, blind pentest and internal test secret." />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="core">Core Concepts</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Honeypot</td><td>Decoy system designed to attract and engage attackers. The reverse proxy diverts suspicious requests to isolated honeypot pool instances running fake WordPress environments.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IDS</td><td>Intrusion Detection System — Suricata network IDS, which generates eve.json alerts parsed by suricata_rules.lua.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SIEM</td><td>Security Information and Event Management — Elasticsearch/Kibana stack for log ingestion and visualization.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE</td><td>Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures identifier — used to name specific seeded vulnerability patterns.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WSTG</td><td>OWASP Web Security Testing Guide — category system used to classify detection patterns.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="detection">Detection Terms</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>SID</td><td>Suricata rule identifier — allocated in suricata.md (1000001-1000070+).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Risk Score</td><td>Accumulated threat score (0-100) computed by pattern_analyzer.analyze_request().</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Honeypot Threshold</td><td>Minimum risk score (default 80) that triggers honeypot routing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decay</td><td>Exponential reduction of stored risk scores over time since last suspicious signal.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Permaflag</td><td>Score stops decaying entirely — triggered by permaflag_offenses (5) or permaflag_score (100).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Offense</td><td>Counter bumped once per recorded attack signal, used by decay_policy to slow decay.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Honeytoken</td><td>Fake high-value artefact (API key, DB credentials, admin password, S3 URL, JWT secret) planted in honeypot responses — a later request carrying one confirms compromise with high confidence.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GDPR Signature</td><td>Consent-gated, privacy-preserving one-way hash of browser characteristics (plus the server-observed IP) used as a second identifier that survives a cookie and storage wipe. See <a href="/docs/detection/signature">Device &amp; GDPR Signature</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sophistication Class</td><td>Weighted classification of a session as <code>scripted</code>, <code>ai_assisted</code>, <code>manual</code> or <code>unknown</code>, produced by <code>sophistication_analyzer.lua</code> with a confidence margin.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>eve.json</code></td><td>Suricata's structured JSON event log; parsed by <code>suricata_rules.lua</code> into alert records and severity-graded score deltas (replaced the older <code>fast.log</code> regex path).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Prompt-Injection Probe</td><td>A check (<code>prompt_injection_filter.lua</code>) that scores attempts to manipulate an LLM-backed consumer of the logs; contributes <code>filter risk / 2</code> to the request score. Run as the 7th scoring signal inside <code>pattern_analyzer.lua</code> — a separate, internal numbering from the <a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">routing pipeline's</a> own Stage 7.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="architecture">Architecture Terms</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Pure Module</td><td>Lua module with no ngx.* dependency, plain-lua-interpreter testable (all *_rules.lua files).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Adapter Module</td><td>Lua module performing I/O, delegating decisions to pure modules.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool</td><td>Isolated honeypot environment (honeypot_eshop_N + honeypot_database_N) assigned per user session. See the <a href="/docs/engine#pools">Engine</a> page.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Per-Session Segmentation</td><td>Telling visitors apart by session (signed cookie + fingerprint) rather than by IP, so several users behind one address — or one clearing cookies — are each isolated on their own pool.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Honeypot Layer</td><td>Where deception happens: reverse-proxy level (separate decoy backends / pools) or database level (one eshop, production vs honeypot database swapped per request). See <a href="/docs/engine/layers">Honeypot Layers</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Escalation</td><td>Gradual increase in response to repeated suspicious signals.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sticky Routing</td><td>Once honeypot_bound, all subsequent requests locked to same honeypot pool.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Static Pool</td><td>A pool (numbers 1..<code>STATIC_POOL_COUNT</code>, default 3) declared in compose and always running, so there is warm capacity before the pool manager builds any runtime pools.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool Autoscaling</td><td>Runtime creation of pools above the static count — one per waiting session plus spares — within a memory / CPU / count budget (<code>POOL_MAX</code>, <code>POOL_MAX_MEMORY_MB</code>, <code>POOL_MAX_CPUS</code>), with idle pools retired after <code>POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS</code>. See the <a href="/docs/engine#pools">Engine</a> page.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="scoring">Scoring Terms</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Stage 6</td><td>IP reputation gate in router.lua — diverts IPs scoring >50 on address's own reputation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shared Address</td><td>Public IP shared by multiple sessions (NAT) — detected via shared_ip_rules.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IP Reputation Cap</td><td>Maximum score an address's reputation can contribute per request on shared addresses (default 10).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decision Trace</td><td>Flat strings logged to Nginx security access log by router_rules.decision_trace(), parsed by Vector into Kibana fields.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="operations">Operations Terms</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Hardening</td><td>Security posture improvements for production deployment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Runbook</td><td>Operational troubleshooting and response procedures.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Blind Pentest</td><td>External attacker assessment where the tester has no prior knowledge of the deployment or routing logic. See <a href="/docs/testing/blind-pentest">Blind Pentest</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Internal Test Secret</td><td>Shared secret (<code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code>) gating the <code>X-Route-Target</code> / <code>X-Risk-Score</code> diagnostic headers; fail closed when unset, so a blind tester cannot read their own routing/score oracle.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SIEM Pipeline</td><td>The log path <code>Nginx / Suricata → Vector → Elasticsearch → Kibana</code>; in a two-host deployment the Vector hop is secured with mutual TLS. See <a href="/docs/observability/siem">SIEM</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Honeypot Layer</td><td>Reverse-proxy deception (separate decoy pools) vs database deception (one eshop, DB swapped per request). See <a href="/docs/engine/layers">Honeypot Layers</a>.</td></tr>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
